---
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro';
const title = "How to Book Hotels in China for Foreigners";
const description = "Book hotels in China as a foreigner — Trip.com, hotel prices, foreign-guest policies, check-in, and where to stay.";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} lang="en">
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-8 not-prose">
      <a href="/en/life/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm text-gray-400 hover:text-gray-700 transition no-underline group">
        <svg class="w-3.5 h-3.5 transition-transform group-hover:-translate-x-0.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7"/>
        </svg>
        Back to Life
      </a>
    </nav>
    <header class="mb-12">
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-3">🏨 Life in China</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">
        How to Book Hotels in China for Foreigners
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-6">Prices, foreign-guest policies, booking platforms, and check-in — what to know before you book.</p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <span>📅 Last updated: August 2026</span>
        <span>⏱️ 6 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-green-50 to-emerald-50 border-l-4 border-green-500 rounded-r-2xl p-8 mb-12 not-prose">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-green-900 mb-4">🎯 Quick Answer</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-green-800 leading-loose">
        Yes, you can book hotels in China as a foreigner — the key is choosing one that accepts foreign guests. Use <strong>Trip.com</strong> (the international site): English interface, passport-based booking, and Visa/Mastercard payment all work. Expect to pay roughly <strong>¥120–¥300 ($18–$44)</strong> for a clean budget chain (Home Inn, Hanting, 7 Days), <strong>¥400–¥900 ($59–$133)</strong> for a mid-range stay (All Seasons, Atour), and <strong>¥900+ ($133+)</strong> for international 4–5 star hotels. Bring your <strong>original passport</strong> — a photo will not work — and be ready for a 5–15 minute check-in while the front desk registers you with the police.
      </p>
    </div>

    <nav class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-12 not-prose">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">📋 In This Guide</h3>
      <ul class="space-y-3">
        <li><a href="#costs" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">💰 How Much Do Hotels Cost in China?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#foreigners" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🛂 Do All Hotels Accept Foreigners?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#platforms" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🏨 Which Booking Platform Should You Use?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#checkin" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">📝 What Happens at Check-In?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#location" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">📍 Where Should You Stay?</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
      <hr class="border-gray-200 my-12" />

      <h2 id="costs" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8 flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="text-4xl">💰</span> How Much Do Hotels Cost in China?
      </h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-12 not-prose">
        <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse bg-white rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
              <th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">Type</th>
              <th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">Typical nightly rate</th>
              <th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">What you get</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-b border-gray-100">
              <td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Budget chain</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">¥120–¥300 ($18–$44)</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">Home Inn (如家), Hanting (汉庭), 7 Days (7天), Jinjiang. Clean, modern, sometimes small rooms. Can drop below ¥100 in smaller cities.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50">
              <td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Mid-range</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">¥400–¥900 ($59–$133)</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">All Seasons (全季), Atour (亚朵), Orange (桔子), Vienna. The sweet spot — feels like a Western 3–4 star.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-gray-100">
              <td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">High-end</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">¥900–¥2,000+ ($133–$296+)</td>
              <td class="px-6 py-4">International chains (Marriott, Hilton, Hyatt, Shangri-La) and domestic luxury brands. Often noticeably cheaper than the same brand in Western cities.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-lg leading-loose text-gray-700 mb-12">Prices spike hard during <strong>National Day Golden Week (early October), Chinese New Year, and summer in tier-1 cities</strong> — a ¥150 budget room can hit ¥900 in central Beijing during peak. Book early or shift dates if you can.</p>

      <hr class="border-gray-200 my-12" />

      <h2 id="foreigners" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8 flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="text-4xl">🛂</span> Do All Hotels Accept Foreigners?
      </h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-loose text-gray-700 mb-10"><strong>Officially, yes — in practice, not always.</strong> A July 2024 notice from seven Chinese ministries told hotels they may not refuse foreign guests for lacking a "foreign guest" license, and asked platforms to stop publishing "no foreigners" listings. Enforcement, however, is uneven: most hotels in big cities take foreigners without issue, but budget chains and small guesthouses — and even different branches of the same chain — vary. A Hanting that accepts foreigners downtown might not accept them in a smaller city.</p>

      <p class="text-lg leading-loose text-gray-700 mb-10"><strong>The reliable workaround:</strong> before making a reservation, you can check the hotel's policy page on Trip — it lists whether the hotel accepts guests from other countries and regions. This way, you can eliminate those hotels that do not accept you. If you do get refused at the desk with a confirmed booking, you have three options: call the bilingual hotline <strong>12367</strong> (National Immigration Administration) or <strong>12345</strong>, or — usually the fastest — walk to the next hotel. Don't let one front desk derail your trip.</p>

      <hr class="border-gray-200 my-12" />

      <h2 id="platforms" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8 flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="text-4xl">🏨</span> Which Booking Platform Should You Use?
      </h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-loose text-gray-700 mb-10"><strong>Trip.com</strong> is the first choice for booking hotels in China: English interface, passport-based booking, foreign-card payment, and the largest selection (over a million properties), with the strongest budget-hotel coverage and the best filters and reviews.</p>

      <div class="bg-yellow-50 border border-yellow-100 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-12">
        <p class="text-gray-700"><strong>🏨 Pro tip:</strong> after searching, flip to the Chinese reviews (Trip.com shows them below the English ones; your phone's translate tool handles the rest). Chinese reviews are usually written by domestic travelers who are pickier about noise, cleanliness, and location — more useful than English reviews from first-time visitors. Look for 4.5+ ratings with a few hundred reviews.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-12 not-prose">
        <p class="text-gray-700"><strong>👉 Book hotels on Trip.com:</strong> <a href="https://www.trip.com/t/Rs0dQkxrzV2" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Search hotels in China</a></p>
      </div>

      <p class="text-lg leading-loose text-gray-700 mb-12"><strong>Booking.com and Agoda</strong> also accept passports and cover Chinese hotels — their listings are similar to each other, skewing toward higher-end properties, and for some upscale hotels the rate is actually below Trip.com. A practical workflow: filter and shortlist on Trip.com first (its filters and Chinese reviews are the most useful), then paste the hotel name into Booking.com and Agoda to compare prices. Booking.com is handy if you have loyalty status with an international chain.</p>

      <hr class="border-gray-200 my-12" />

      <h2 id="checkin" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8 flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="text-4xl">📝</span> What Happens at Check-In?
      </h2>

      <ul class="space-y-6 mb-12 list-none pl-0">
        <li>
          <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Original passport, no exceptions.</p>
          <div class="text-lg text-gray-700">The law requires hotels to verify and register your identity, so a copy or photo on your phone will not be accepted.</div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Police registration is done for you.</p>
          <div class="text-lg text-gray-700">Under the 24-hour registration rule, the front desk registers your stay with the local police automatically. Ask for your <strong>Registration Form of Temporary Residence</strong> (临时住宿登记表) — a printed slip that costs nothing and matters if you later apply for a visa extension.</div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Police may visit to verify your documents.</p>
          <div class="text-lg text-gray-700">Under the same rule, officers sometimes stop by the hotel — often at breakfast — to check foreign guests' passports in person. It's routine, not a raid. Keep your passport with you for the first day or two after check-in, and hand it over when asked.</div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">A refundable deposit</p>
          <div class="text-lg text-gray-700">Some hotels skip it entirely, others ask for ¥200–¥500. WeChat Pay and Alipay both work for the hold, and it refunds automatically to the original account after checkout.</div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Wi-Fi caveat</p>
          <div class="text-lg text-gray-700">Hotel Wi-Fi routes through mainland networks, so your usual apps (Google, WhatsApp, Instagram) may not load on it. Keep your eSIM/roaming data on for international apps and use hotel Wi-Fi for local browsing — see the <a href="/en/life/sim-card-guide/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">SIM &amp; eSIM guide</a> for how to stay connected.</div>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <hr class="border-gray-200 my-12" />

      <h2 id="location" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8 flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="text-4xl">📍</span> Where Should You Stay?
      </h2>

      <p class="text-lg leading-loose text-gray-700 mb-10"><strong>Near a metro station.</strong> Chinese cities are huge and rush-hour taxis crawl; being within 500m of a metro exit saves you an hour a day.</p>

      <p class="text-lg leading-loose text-gray-700 mb-10"><strong>Near your arrival airport or high-speed rail station</strong> is also worth considering if it's close to the main sights — the transfer is easier on day one, and after checkout you can leave your bags at the hotel and pick them up on the way to your next city instead of hauling them across town. Two more habits that pay off:</p>

      <ul class="space-y-6 mb-12 list-none pl-0">
        <li>
          <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Screenshot your hotel's Chinese name and address.</p>
          <div class="text-lg text-gray-700">Do it before you arrive — taxi and DiDi drivers read Chinese instantly but may not recognize the English name at all.</div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Use the payment guides first.</p>
          <div class="text-lg text-gray-700">If your hotel only takes Alipay/WeChat Pay, you'll want those set up before check-in — see <a href="/en/payment/payment-guide/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">How to Pay in China</a>.</div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Need a hand picking a hotel?</p>
          <div class="text-lg text-gray-700">Drop me a line — happy to suggest options based on your itinerary.</div>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-lg leading-loose text-gray-700 mb-12">A safe default in every city: the old center near a metro interchange — walkable, well-connected, and where most of the headline sights cluster. Beyond that, the best neighborhoods differ by city, and each city guide on this site covers where to stay in detail.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-8 not-prose">
      <div class="border-t border-gray-100 pt-6 mt-8">
        <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-brand-50 to-blue-50 border border-brand-100 rounded-xl p-5 not-prose">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 flex-wrap">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">📚 Next Reads</h3>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
              <a href="/en/transport/transport-guide/" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-1.5 px-4 py-2.5 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl text-sm font-medium text-gray-900 hover:border-brand-300 hover:shadow-sm transition no-underline">
                🚗 How to Get Around China
                <svg class="w-3.5 h-3.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg>
              </a>
              <a href="/en/transport/high-speed-rail/" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-1.5 px-4 py-2.5 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl text-sm font-medium text-gray-900 hover:border-brand-300 hover:shadow-sm transition no-underline">
                🚄 High-Speed Rail Guide
                <svg class="w-3.5 h-3.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg>
              </a>
              <a href="/en/payment/payment-guide/" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-1.5 px-4 py-2.5 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl text-sm font-medium text-gray-900 hover:border-brand-300 hover:shadow-sm transition no-underline">
                💳 How to Pay in China
                <svg class="w-3.5 h-3.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg>
              </a>
              <a href="/en/life/sim-card-guide/" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-1.5 px-4 py-2.5 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl text-sm font-medium text-gray-900 hover:border-brand-300 hover:shadow-sm transition no-underline">
                📶 SIM & eSIM Guide
                <svg class="w-3.5 h-3.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg>
              </a>
              <a href="/en/payment/china-travel-budget/" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-1.5 px-4 py-2.5 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl text-sm font-medium text-gray-900 hover:border-brand-300 hover:shadow-sm transition no-underline">
                💰 Trip to China Cost 2026
                <svg class="w-3.5 h-3.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg>
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 not-prose">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-400 uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">Sources</h3>
      <ol class="space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <li>1. <strong>Seven-ministry notice (July 2024)</strong> — hotels may not refuse foreign guests; platforms to remove "no foreigners" listings: <a href="https://www.gov.cn/zhengce/zhengceku/202407/content_6964443.htm" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">gov.cn</a></li>
        <li>2. <strong>Exit-Entry Administration Law, Article 39</strong> — 24-hour accommodation registration requirement: <a href="https://www.nia.gov.cn/n794014/n1050181/n1050484/c1771556/content.html" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">nia.gov.cn</a></li>
        <li>3. <strong>National Immigration Administration hotline 12367</strong> — bilingual, 24/7, for hotel refusal and registration questions: <a href="https://s.nia.gov.cn/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">s.nia.gov.cn</a></li>
      </ol>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mt-4">Prices and policies verified August 2026; they change seasonally and by branch — confirm before you book.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
